---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "How to Store a Portable Generator", url: "/how-to-store-a-portable-generator/" },
    { title: "Generator Runs But No Power", url: "/generator-runs-but-no-power/" },
    { title: "Best Portable Generators", url: "/best-portable-generator/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-10", modified: "2026-09-10" });
const title = "How Often to Change Generator Oil (Hours and Grades)";
const description =
  "First change at 20-30 hours, then every 50-200 depending on the engine. The real intervals, the right grade for cold weather, and how to do it in fifteen minutes.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "How Often to Change Generator Oil", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-often-to-change-generator-oil/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should you change the oil in a generator?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "The first change comes early, at roughly 20 to 30 hours, to flush out metal particles from the engine breaking in. After that, most portable generators call for a change every 50 to 100 hours of running, and premium inverter and standby engines often extend that to 100 to 200 hours. Regardless of hours, change it at least once a year, because oil degrades on the calendar as well as under load." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you need to change generator oil after an outage?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "If a multi-day outage put more than 50 hours on the machine, yes. A three-day outage running around the clock is roughly 72 hours, which exceeds a typical portable's whole service interval in one event. Do it once power is back rather than waiting for the next storm, because a generator with tired oil sitting for a year is how bearings get damaged." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What kind of oil does a portable generator use?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "A detergent oil meeting the API service class in your manual, usually SAE 30 for warm-weather use or 10W-30 for a wider range, with 5W-30 synthetic specified by many manufacturers for cold-weather running. Small air-cooled engines are not the place to improvise. Use what the manual lists, and if the machine runs in winter, follow the cold-weather grade rather than the summer one." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use car oil in a generator?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Generally yes, if the grade and API class match what the manual specifies — most small-engine oil is ordinary automotive oil in a smaller bottle. What matters is avoiding energy-conserving formulations and unusual grades that were never intended for an air-cooled engine, and never using two-stroke oil in a four-stroke generator. When in doubt, buy the grade printed in the manual." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much oil does a generator take?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Most portable generators hold between about 0.5 and 1.2 litres, and larger frame units more. The number is in the manual, but the reliable method is to fill slowly and check the dipstick or fill-neck level rather than measuring by the bottle. Overfilling an air-cooled engine causes smoking, oil in the air filter and poor running, so it is a real risk, not a theoretical one." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What happens if you never change generator oil?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "The oil loses its additive package, its detergents stop holding contamination in suspension, and combustion acids attack bearing surfaces. In an air-cooled engine that runs hot, the result is accelerated wear on the crank and rod bearings and eventually a rod failure or a seized engine. On a machine that only ran a few hours a year, the failure often surfaces during the outage you bought it for." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should you change generator oil before or after storage?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Before. Used oil carries acids and moisture that keep working on internal surfaces for the whole time the machine sits, so draining it warm at the end of the season is the better habit. It also means the generator is ready to run when a storm arrives rather than needing service first, which is the whole point of owning one." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do inverter generators need oil changes too?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Yes. An inverter generator still has a four-stroke engine with the same needs, and the electronics that make it an inverter change nothing about lubrication. Access is often more awkward because of the enclosed body, and many models use a small drain plug or require tilting the unit, so check the procedure in the manual before starting rather than mid-job." },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Generators & Backup Power",
    tags: ["generator oil change", "generator maintenance", "oil grade", "small engine"],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Portable Generators", link: "/best-portable-generator/" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6"><TrustBadge /></div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">Maintenance</span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">Field Tested</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">How Often to Change <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Generator Oil</span></h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          First change at 20 to 30 hours, then every 50 to 200 depending on the engine — and at least annually whatever the hour meter says.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#intervals" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">See the Intervals</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">Jump to the FAQ</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto"><SocialProof users="7.2k" /></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">In This Guide</h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#intervals" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Intervals by Machine Type</a>
          <a href="#break-in" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Break-In Change</a>
          <a href="#calendar" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Hours vs the Calendar</a>
          <a href="#grade" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Choosing the Right Grade</a>
          <a href="#how-to" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Doing the Change</a>
          <a href="#outages" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Long Outages and Mid-Storm Changes</a>
          <a href="#warning-signs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Warning Signs You Left It Too Long</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A generator engine has no oil filter on most portable machines, no coolant, and no ability to complain. The oil is the entire maintenance system, and it is doing that job in an air-cooled engine that runs hot and often at a steady heavy load for days on end.
        </p>

        <div id="intervals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Intervals by Machine Type</h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Machine</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">First Change</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Then Every</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Budget portable, no filter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mid-range portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-30 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50-100 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Inverter generator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-30 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Premium engine with filter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25-50 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-200 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Standby (whole house)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per commissioning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-200 hours or annually</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Your manual wins any disagreement with this table, because the figures vary by engine family rather than by brand badge. What the table gets right is the shape of the thing: cheap engines with no oil filter need attention far more often than premium ones, and everything needs the early break-in change.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note also that these are running hours, not elapsed days. A generator that has been exercised for thirty minutes a month for two years has fewer than twelve hours on it. One that carried a house through a four-day ice storm has close to a hundred.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="break-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Break-In Change</h2>

          <p>
            New engines shed metal. Piston rings bed into cylinder walls, bearings take their final shape, and machining residue works loose — and in an engine without an oil filter, all of that circulates in suspension until it is drained out.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the first change comes so early, typically at 20 to 30 hours, and it is the one interval people most often skip because the machine is new and appears fine. It is also the highest-value oil change in the machine's life, since the debris it removes is the debris that would otherwise be lapping the bearings for the next fifty hours.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">A Practical Break-In Routine</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Run the new machine at a moderate load — around half rated output — for its first few hours, then change the oil at 20 to 25 hours and settle into the normal interval.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Avoid both extremes during break-in: a brand-new engine idling with no load for hours glazes its bores, and one hammered at full output from cold does not do the rings any favours either. A steady moderate load is what seats them properly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="calendar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Hours vs the Calendar</h2>

          <p>
            Most home generators never come close to their hour interval, which is precisely the problem. A machine that runs twelve hours a year will hit fifty hours some time in its fifth season, and by then the oil has spent four winters absorbing moisture and holding combustion acids against bearing surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the rule has two halves: whichever comes first, hours or a year. For most households that means an annual oil change, done at the end of storm season or before winter, whichever suits the climate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep an hour meter if the machine has one, and a notebook if it does not. Writing the date and rough hours on a strip of tape on the frame after every run takes ten seconds and is far more reliable than remembering, especially since the person changing the oil next year may not be the person who ran it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Choosing the Right Grade</h2>

          <p>
            The grade is where cold weather makes this more than a routine job. An air-cooled generator engine running in winter has to be turned over by a recoil starter or a small electric starter, and oil that is too thick makes that genuinely difficult.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Temperature Range</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Grade</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Above 4°C</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">SAE 30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Warm-weather standard</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">−18°C to 38°C</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10W-30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The all-round choice</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Below freezing, regularly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5W-30 synthetic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Much easier cold starts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Severe cold</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per manual, often 5W-30 synthetic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check before assuming</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Synthetic is worth the difference in a generator, not because the machine needs exotic lubrication but because its cold-flow behaviour is genuinely better and because a generator's duty cycle — long, hot, steady-load runs punctuated by months of sitting — is the sort of use that punishes conventional oil hardest.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caveat: some manufacturers advise keeping conventional oil during the break-in period, on the theory that synthetic's lower friction slows ring seating. Follow the manual rather than the internet on that point, then switch afterwards if you want to.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your machine is a dual-fuel or propane unit, note that running on propane burns cleaner and produces less oil contamination than gasoline. That does not extend the interval as far as people assume — the thermal and calendar ageing still applies — but oil from a propane-fed engine does typically come out looking noticeably cleaner. Our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">dual-fuel generator testing</a> covers the practical differences.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Doing the Change</h2>

          <p>
            Run the engine for five minutes first. Warm oil is thinner, flows out completely, and carries suspended contamination with it rather than leaving it settled in the sump. Then shut it down, disconnect the spark plug lead, and let it cool enough to handle safely — warm, not hot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Position the machine so the drain plug is over your pan, and be honest about how awkward this is on most portables. A short length of tubing on the drain, or a purpose-made oil-change funnel, saves a lot of cleanup. On many inverter models the manual specifies tilting the unit rather than using a drain plug — check that before starting, not after the oil is already on the ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drain it fully, refit the plug with its washer, and refill slowly. Check the level the way your manual specifies, which on many small engines means the dipstick resting on the threads rather than screwed in, and always on level ground. Overfilling an air-cooled engine causes smoking, oil pushed into the air filter, and poor running.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the machine has an oil filter, change it at the same time and pre-fill it if it mounts vertically. Then run the engine two minutes, shut it down, and re-check the level once the oil has drained back — it will read lower than it did.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dispose of the used oil at a garage or household hazardous waste facility. A litre of used engine oil is a genuine contaminant, and every jurisdiction has somewhere that takes it free.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Long Outages and Mid-Storm Changes</h2>

          <p>
            This is the scenario that catches people out. A three-day outage running a generator continuously puts about 72 hours on the engine — more than a whole service interval for many portables, in a single event.
          </p>

          <p>
            If an outage is going to run long, plan an oil change during it. Keep a litre of the correct oil, a drain pan and a wrench with the machine specifically for this. Shut the generator down when the household load is naturally low, let it cool, change the oil, and restart. Twenty minutes without power is a much smaller cost than a damaged engine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the level daily during extended running, too. Small air-cooled engines consume some oil under sustained heavy load, and a machine that started the outage at the full mark can be meaningfully down by day three. Low oil is what triggers the low-oil shutdown that people mistake for a fault.
          </p>

          <p>
            And when power returns, change the oil before putting the machine away, even if you changed it mid-outage. It has just done more work in a week than it usually does in five years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warning-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Warning Signs You Left It Too Long</h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Very dark, thin oil on the dipstick.</strong> Darkening is normal — detergent oil holds contamination in suspension, which is its job. Oil that is black and noticeably thinner than fresh oil has lost viscosity and its additive package, and is no longer protecting much of anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A milky or coffee-coloured emulsion.</strong> That is water in the oil, from condensation during storage or from very short runs that never brought the engine up to temperature. Change it, then run the machine long enough to reach full operating temperature at least once a month rather than starting it for five minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A gritty feel between finger and thumb.</strong> Metal in suspension, and in an engine without a filter that grit is going through the bearings. Change it immediately, and consider a second change after a short run to flush the rest.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Low-oil shutdowns.</strong> Most generators have a sensor that stops the engine on low oil. If yours trips, do not top up and carry on without asking where the oil went — consumption that high points at wear, and a machine that shuts down at the start of an outage is a machine that needs attention before the next one. If it cuts out for other reasons, our guide to <a href="/generator-runs-but-no-power/">a generator that runs but makes no power</a> covers the electrical side.
          </p>
        </div>


        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Filters, Fuel Type and Standby Units</h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Machines With an Oil Filter</h3>
          <p>
            Most budget portables have no oil filter at all, which is the single biggest reason their intervals are short — every particle the engine sheds stays in circulation until you drain it. Premium engines and most standby units do have one, and that is where the longer 100 to 200 hour intervals come from.
          </p>
          <p>
            If yours has a filter, change it with every oil change rather than every second one. It is an inexpensive part, and a filter left in place holds a meaningful volume of dirty oil that immediately contaminates the fresh charge. Pre-fill it where it mounts vertically, smear clean oil on the gasket, and tighten by hand to the turns marked on the filter rather than with a wrench.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane and Natural Gas</h3>
          <p>
            Gaseous fuels burn far more cleanly than gasoline. There is no unburned fuel washing down the cylinder walls diluting the oil, and far less carbon and acid production, so oil from a propane-fed engine genuinely does come out looking cleaner at the same hours.
          </p>
          <p>
            That does not extend the interval as far as people hope, though. Thermal breakdown of the oil and calendar ageing continue regardless of what is burning, and the manufacturer's figure already accounts for the fuel the machine is rated on. Treat clean-looking oil on a dual-fuel machine as a sign the engine is healthy, not as permission to skip a change.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Standby Generators</h3>
          <p>
            A whole-house standby unit exercises itself weekly, typically for ten to twenty minutes, which accumulates roughly ten to fifteen hours a year before a single outage. Most manufacturers specify an annual service regardless, and many make it a condition of the warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those services usually bundle oil, oil filter, air filter and spark plugs, and on a liquid-cooled unit the coolant as well. If you service it yourself, keep the record — a warranty claim on a standby unit with no maintenance history is a difficult conversation. Our guide to <a href="/whole-house-generator-installation-cost/">standby installation costs</a> covers what ongoing servicing typically runs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reading the Dipstick Properly</h3>
          <p>
            Two details catch people out on small engines. First, many manufacturers specify that the dipstick is rested on the threads rather than screwed in when reading — screwing it in on those engines gives a reading well above the true level, and people then drain oil they needed. Check the manual.
          </p>
          <p>
            Second, always read on level ground with the engine off and a few minutes elapsed. A generator parked on a slope during an outage can read half a centimetre out, which on a sump holding under a litre is a significant fraction of the total.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Break-In Change</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The 20 to 30 hour change removes break-in metal that would otherwise circulate through unfiltered bearings for the next fifty hours. It is the highest-value oil change the engine ever gets.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting Only Hours, Never Years</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A generator run twelve hours a year takes five seasons to reach fifty hours, by which time the oil has spent four winters absorbing moisture. Change it annually regardless of the meter.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Summer Oil in a Winter Machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Straight SAE 30 below freezing is thick enough to make a recoil start genuinely hard and to slow oil reaching the bearings on startup. Use the cold-weather grade the manual specifies, usually 5W-30 synthetic.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overfilling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Air-cooled engines are intolerant of too much oil: smoking, oil in the air filter and poor running follow. Fill slowly, check on level ground, and use the dipstick method your manual specifies.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Three Days Straight Without a Change</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 72-hour outage exceeds a typical portable's entire service interval. Keep a litre of oil, a pan and a wrench with the machine, and plan a mid-outage change rather than hoping.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing It in Spring Instead of Autumn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Used oil holds acids and moisture that keep working while the machine sits. Drain it warm at the end of the season, so the generator both stores better and is ready to run when it is needed.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often, in hours?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">First change at 20 to 30 hours, then every 50 to 100 on most portables and 100 to 200 on premium and inverter engines. And at least annually regardless of hours, because oil ages on the calendar too.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">After a long outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Yes, if it ran more than about 50 hours — and a three-day outage is roughly 72. Change it once power is back rather than storing the machine on tired oil for a year.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What oil does it take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">A detergent oil in the grade and API class the manual lists — usually SAE 30 for warm weather, 10W-30 as an all-rounder, and 5W-30 synthetic for cold-weather running. Follow the manual, not habit.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use car oil?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Generally yes if the grade and API class match — most small-engine oil is automotive oil in a smaller bottle. Avoid energy-conserving formulations and odd grades, and never put two-stroke oil in a four-stroke generator.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Most portables take 0.5 to 1.2 litres, larger frames more. The manual gives the figure, but fill slowly and confirm on the dipstick — overfilling an air-cooled engine causes smoking and oil in the air filter.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What if you never change it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">The additives deplete, detergents stop holding contamination, and combustion acids attack the bearings. In a hot air-cooled engine that means accelerated wear and eventually a rod failure — often during the outage you bought the machine for.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Before or after storage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Before. Used oil carries acids and moisture that keep working the whole time it sits, and a machine stored on fresh oil is ready to run when a storm arrives rather than needing service first.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do inverter generators need it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Yes — same four-stroke engine, same needs. Access is often more awkward inside the enclosed body, and some models require tilting rather than a drain plug, so read the procedure before you start the job.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

