            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Break-in change at 20 to 30 hours, then every 50 to 100 on most portables</strong> — more like 100 to 200 on premium and inverter engines with filters. Whichever comes first, hours or twelve months.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Use the grade your manual specifies for the temperature you actually run in, which for winter use usually means 5W-30 synthetic rather than summer-weight SAE 30. Drain it warm, fill it slowly, and check the level on level ground.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And plan for the long outage: a three-day run exceeds a whole service interval, so keep oil, a pan and a wrench with the machine, watch the level daily, and change it again before it goes back into storage.
          </p>
        </div>

